Caveman is a moderately difficult role, IMO.  I consider it a
more difficult role than barbarian, for example.

> uphill struggle (around the time you first reach the Quest portal)
> until you get a good artifact weapon.

A lawful caveman can dip for Excalibur.  Being restricted
in longsword makes this a less attractive weapon than for
some of the other roles, but the +1d5 to-hit bonus helps,
and by the time you're finding the quest portal you should
be high-level enough to hit reasonably frequently with it.
It'll probably do okay for the pre-Castle game.

Once you get the Castle wand, you can either wish for
the weapon you really want, or if you don't want to use
a wish on a weapon you can sacrifice to get your
longsword skill unrestricted.

I'm not sure what to do about mid-game weapons for
a neutral caveman.  I suppose you could use an
enchanted unicorn horn for a while.  Probably don't
want to go into Gehennom that way, though.
Magicbane, perhaps?  Vorpal blade?

The major late-game Caveman challenges, IMO, are
the inability to dual-wield and the difficulty of casting
spells.  However, most of the roles that don't have
the latter problem have a tough early game.

Valkyries are a notable exception.  In the late game
(with a robe and a helm of brilliance) they can cast
spells up to level 3 with 100% success (level 3 being
particularly important, as it houses the identify and
remove curse and charm monster spells, three of the
five or so most useful spells in the game), and they
also have the easiest early game of any role, IMO.

Knights are another exception.  They can cast
better than Valkyries, and their early game, though
harder than for Valkyries, is clearly easier than for
Cavemen.  Samurai are perhaps somewhere in
between -- easier early game than Knights, same
spellcasting ability as Valkyries.

But apart from those three, most of the roles that
can cast spells effectively have a tougher early game
than Cavemen.

Dual-wielding is another matter.  Barbarians, for
example, can do so with basic proficiency, and
Rogues can make Expert in it.  The fact that
Cavemen cannot is a real handicap for them in
the late game.  Arguably, this increases the
importance of getting a good primary weapon
and enchanting it as high as possible.

